[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 764,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 701,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120715,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 701,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Gamma heavy chain disease (HCD) is a B-cell lymphoplasmacytic neoplasm exhibiting variable morphology with nodal, bone marrow, and extranodal involvement. All cases secrete an M&nbsp;protein characterized as gamma heavy chain, without associated light chains.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 765,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 702,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120716,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 702,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>9762/3 Gamma heavy chain disease</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 766,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 703,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120717,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 703,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>2A84.1 Gamma heavy chain disease</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 767,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 704,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120718,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 704,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> Franklin disease.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 768,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 705,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120719,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 705,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 769,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 706,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120720,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 706,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Most patients have disseminated nodal disease, with or without splenomegaly or marrow involvement. Localized extranodal disease may be limited to the bone marrow or another site, most commonly the skin.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 770,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 707,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120721,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 707,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Underlying autoimmune disease (present in a subset of patients) generally precedes the onset of HCD by several years {2509855; 12861101}. Cytopenias are related to underlying autoimmunity or bone marrow infiltration.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 771,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 708,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120722,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 708,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>HCD is a rare disease, with a female predominance; patients are in their sixth or seventh decade of life {2509855; 12861101}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 772,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 709,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120723,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 709,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The association with an underlying autoimmune disease in many cases suggests an etiological link to lymphoma development, but further studies are needed {2509855; 12861101}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 773,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 710,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120724,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 710,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Gamma HCD arises from a clonal expansion of cells producing abnormal truncated gamma heavy chain that lacks light chain binding sites {18434654}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 780,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 717,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120725,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 717,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Enlarged lymph nodes and spleen</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 774,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 711,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120726,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 711,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The morphological findings are heterogeneous. In lymph nodes, most cases contain an interfollicular or diffuse infiltrate of small lymphocytes, plasmacytoid lymphocytes, plasma cells, and admixed immunoblasts and histiocytes, resembling a polymorphous lymphoplasmacytic or (nodal and extranodal) marginal zone lymphoma. In biopsies with features of extranodal marginal zone lymphoma, lymphoepithelial lesions and follicular colonization may be present. Monocytoid differentiation is typically absent {22301495}. In some cases, increased eosinophils and hypervascularity raise the differential diagnosis of Hodgkin or peripheral T-cell lymphoma {18434654}. Transformation to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma may occur {16026747; 24683718}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 775,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 712,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120727,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 712,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Cytological sampling may be used for primary diagnosis in conjunction with flow cytometry, immunofixation, or molecular testing {25180407}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 776,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 713,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120728,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 713,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Clonal IG gene rearrangements are found. No known recurrent genetic mutations are known {22301495}. <em>MYD88</em> is wildtype {24859878}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 779,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 716,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120729,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 716,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> serum or urine immunofixation electrophoresis demonstrating a gamma monoclonal band without an associated light chain (this may be the only manifestation in patients with autoimmune disease).</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> FNA or tissue biopsy demonstrating atypical lymphoplasmacytic proliferation expressing IgG, typically without light chains.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 777,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 714,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120730,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 714,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Staging follows the Lugano modification of the Ann Arbor staging system for the underlying lymphoma.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 778,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 715,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120731,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 715,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Gamma HCD has a highly variable clinical course, with overall survival ranging from &lt;&nbsp;1 to 20&nbsp;years. Disseminated or medullary disease requires systemic chemotherapy, whereas surgery or radiation is effective for localized extranodal disease {24683718}. Rituximab is recommended for CD20-positive disease, and durable responses have been reported with the use of novel agents, including bendamustine, lenalidomide, and bortezomib {32245744}. Treatment of the underlying autoimmune disease is indicated.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:02:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]